/**
 * factories, long-form SEO overrides
 */
module.exports = {
  "nacsa": "<p><strong>NACSA</strong> (Nicaraguan American Cigars S.A.) is an Estelí factory with multiple owners. One of them is Tampa leaf company Oliva Tobacco Co., which calls its stake \"very small\" and is unrelated to <a href=\"/node/oliva/\">Oliva Cigar Co.</a> Until the early 2010s NACSA was among Nicaragua's largest factories by output, dominated by bundle cigars such as <a href=\"/node/jcnewman/\">J.C. Newman</a>'s Quorum. In 2011 the Newmans opened their own Estelí factory, PENSA, and began moving that production out.</p>\n<p>NACSA then moved toward premium work. In 2015 it moved into a new factory nearby, where Asylum headlined production and newer clients included Acme Cigar Co. and <a href=\"/node/stevesaka/\">Steve Saka</a>. Saka's <a href=\"/node/dunbarton/\">Dunbarton Tobacco &amp; Trust</a> names NACSA and <a href=\"/node/joyafactory/\">Joya de Nicaragua</a> as the two factories where all its marcas are made, with dedicated teams at both. In 2016 industry veteran Larry Palombo joined to launch NACSA's own brands.</p>\n<p>In 2024 <a href=\"/node/warped/\">Warped Cigars</a> began making cigars at NACSA, starting with Sarto, and founder <a href=\"/node/kylegellis/\">Kyle Gellis</a> oversees that production closely. <a href=\"/node/crownedheads/\">Crowned Heads</a> also moved its Le Pâtissier line to NACSA, and the map lists some production for <a href=\"/node/westtampa/\">West Tampa Tobacco Co.</a></p>",
  "lazona": "<p><strong>La Zona</strong> is the Estelí factory <a href=\"/node/erikespinosa/\">Erik Espinosa</a> opened in 2012, after the split of <a href=\"/node/eobrands/\">EO Brands</a>, his partnership with <a href=\"/node/eddieortega/\">Eddie Ortega</a>. It sits in a three-story former colonial-style inn in downtown Estelí. La Zona bunches in the entubado style and finishes cigars with triple caps, and Cigar Coop named it Small Factory of the Year in 2015 and 2016.</p>\n<p>The factory began with the brands Espinosa took from EO. <a href=\"/node/sixzeroone/\">601</a> and <a href=\"/node/murcielago/\">Murciélago</a> came to La Zona after leaving My Father and were made there until both moved to <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernández</a> around 2017. Today La Zona makes the house lines of <a href=\"/node/espinosa/\">Espinosa Premium Cigars</a>, including <a href=\"/node/espinosahabano/\">Espinosa Habano</a>, <a href=\"/node/laranja/\">Laranja Reserva</a> and <a href=\"/node/crema/\">Crema</a>. Crema was the factory's first Connecticut-wrapped Espinosa cigar, launched in 2016. Hector Alfonso Sr., then operations manager, described it as a \"gateway cigar.\"</p>\n<p>La Zona also rolls for outside brands, a common way for a small Estelí factory to fill capacity. Clients have included Arandoza, Flor de Estelí (MoyaRuiz), Cornelius &amp; Anthony, Protocol, La Sirena and Catelli. That mix of house lines and contract work puts La Zona in the same small, owner-run tier as <a href=\"/node/garmendia/\">Garmendia</a> and <a href=\"/node/tabacaleraaragon/\">Tabacalera Aragón</a>, two other Estelí workshops on the map.</p>",
  "ajfesteli": "<p><strong>Tabacalera A.J. Fernández</strong> is <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernández</a>'s main factory in Estelí, Nicaragua. Fernández, a Cuban-born blender, started in 2004 with six rollers in a garage. According to Cigar Journal, that grew to seven factories. His San Lotano brand became the first of his own in 2010. Cigar Coop dates the consolidation of his Estelí operation in this factory to 2013, and another account says it opened in July 2013.</p>\n<p>It is one of the busiest factories in Estelí. Cigar Aficionado reported in 2016 that it was at full capacity, and halfwheel named it Factory of the Year for 2018. halfwheel's listings show it making Fernández's own brands as well as cigars for many other companies. These include <a href=\"/node/altadis/\">Altadis U.S.A.</a>, for <a href=\"/node/trinidadespiritu/\">Trinidad Espiritu</a>, <a href=\"/node/montecristonc/\">Montecristo</a>'s Nicaragua Series and <a href=\"/node/hupmannnc/\">H. Upmann</a>. Other clients include <a href=\"/node/generalcigar/\">General Cigar</a>, <a href=\"/node/caldwell/\">Caldwell</a>, <a href=\"/node/foundation/\">Foundation</a> (Tabernacle, Olmec), <a href=\"/node/lapalina/\">La Palina</a>, <a href=\"/node/southerndraw/\">Southern Draw</a> and <a href=\"/node/agingroom/\">Aging Room</a>. The newer Sunday Gravy releases of <a href=\"/node/diesel/\">Diesel</a>, a Cigars International brand, are also made here.</p>\n<p>In 2017 Fernández opened a second factory, <a href=\"/node/sanlotano/\">San Lotano</a>, in Ocotal north of Estelí. That is where Man O' War, Murciélago and Knuckle Sandwich are made. Cigar Nexus used to lump both factories together under San Lotano. They now have separate nodes, and each link points to the factory that sources name.</p>",
  "sanlotano": "<p><strong>The San Lotano factory</strong> is <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernández</a>'s second Nicaraguan factory. It is in Ocotal, in the municipality of Totogalpa, about 40 miles north of his main factory, Tabacalera A.J. Fernández in Estelí. It used to be the San Rafael factory owned by Fidel Olivas, which made El Baton and <a href=\"/node/brickhouse/\">Brick House</a> for J.C. Newman. Fernández bought it in October 2016, remodeled it, and opened it officially on January 3, 2017. It is named for the brand his grandfather, Andrés Fernández, started in Cuba.</p>\n<p>Fernández bought it because the Estelí factory was at full capacity, and his father, Ismael, ran the new one. The 117,000-square-foot building had room for 240 rollers. A month in, 59 pairs were making 17,000 cigars a day, toward a goal of 30,000. By early 2017 it was making the entire Man O' War and Ave Maria lines for Cigars International, plus Enclave Broadleaf, Free World and Días de Gloria, and <a href=\"/node/diesel/\">Diesel</a> was also planned there.</p>\n<p>Contract work came quickly. Erik Espinosa moved <a href=\"/node/sixzeroone/\">601</a> and <a href=\"/node/murcielago/\">Murciélago</a> here, and <a href=\"/node/knucklesandwich/\">Knuckle Sandwich</a> followed. halfwheel says the factory also makes cigars for <a href=\"/node/altadis/\">Altadis U.S.A.</a> It tied for halfwheel's 2024 Factory of the Year with <a href=\"/node/nicasueno/\">NicaSueño</a>. Cigars that halfwheel lists at Tabacalera AJ Fernandez Cigars de Nicaragua in Estelí, such as Trinidad Espiritu and Southern Draw, now link to <a href=\"/node/ajfesteli/\">Tabacalera A.J. Fernández</a> instead.</p>",
  "tabsa": "<p><strong>TABSA</strong>, short for Tabacos Valle de Jalapa S.A., is the Estelí factory of <a href=\"/node/aganorsa/\">Aganorsa Leaf</a>. Both belong to Eduardo Fernández Pujals. He created the farming company Agrícola Ganadera Norteña S.A. (Aganorsa) in the summer of 1997, starting with about 168 acres mostly in Jalapa. A Nicaraguan university study dates TABSA's first production to 2000. Fernández himself has said it opened in 2002, closed for a while, then reopened, and that in between he had cigars made at <a href=\"/node/raicescubanas/\">Raíces Cubanas</a> with his tobacco.</p>\n<p>In 2002 Fernández also bought Tropical Tobacco (Tabacalera Tropical) in Miami from Pedro Martín. For years the cigar side was sold as Casa Fernández. In April 2018 it was renamed Aganorsa Leaf to tie the cigars to the leaf, which the company grows in Jalapa, Condega and Estelí and sells to many other makers. The factory is run by Fernández's son <a href=\"/node/maxfernandez/\">Max Fernández</a>.</p>\n<p>TABSA is best known for cigars sold under other names. Its clients have included <a href=\"/node/illusione/\">Illusione</a>, <a href=\"/node/viaje/\">Viaje</a>, <a href=\"/node/warped/\">Warped</a> (Flor del Valle and Futuro), early <a href=\"/node/foundation/\">Foundation</a> El Güegüense and <a href=\"/node/hvc/\">HVC</a>, whose cigars were rolled there until HVC opened its own factory in 2022. HVC's San Isidro made Cigar Snob's Top 25 for 2017 as a TABSA cigar. The factory also makes Aganorsa Leaf's own house brands.</p>",
  "joyafactory": "<p>The <strong>Joya de Nicaragua factory</strong> in Estelí makes <a href=\"/node/joya/\">Joya de Nicaragua</a>, Nicaragua's oldest cigar brand, along with contract cigars for brands such as <a href=\"/node/dunbarton/\">Dunbarton</a>'s <a href=\"/node/sobremesa/\">Sobremesa</a>. It is owned by the family of <a href=\"/node/alejandromartinez/\">Alejandro Martínez Cuenca</a>.</p>\n<p>The company was founded in 1968 as the Nicaragua Cigar Co. by the Cuban exiles Juan Francisco Bermejo and Simón Camacho, and Joya was the first cigar brand made in Nicaragua. The dictator Anastasio Somoza Debayle later took control of it. After the Sandinista revolution the company fell into disarray, and during the U.S. embargo of the 1980s production moved to <a href=\"/node/plasencia/\">Nestor Plasencia</a>'s facilities in Honduras.</p>\n<p>In 1994 Martínez Cuenca, a former Sandinista trade minister, bought the company. He rehired veteran rollers, released Antaño 1970, and later bought back the U.S. trademark from Altadis. House lines include Antaño, Clásico, Cinco Décadas, Número Uno and Rosalones. <a href=\"/node/drewestate/\">Drew Estate</a> distributed Joya in the U.S. until the end of 2025, when distribution moved to Quesada's SAG Imports.</p>\n<p>The factory is also a busy contract maker. Dunbarton names Joya and <a href=\"/node/nacsa/\">NACSA</a> as the two factories that make all its marcas. Dunbarton's founder, Steve Saka, had been CEO of Drew Estate, which distributed Joya at the time. The factory also makes Viaje and Fratello lines for their owners.</p>",
  "tabafuente": "<p><strong>Tabacalera A. Fuente y Cía.</strong> is the Fuente family's factory complex in Santiago de los Caballeros, Dominican Republic. <a href=\"/node/carlitofuente/\">Carlos \"Carlito\" Fuente Jr.</a> and his father, Carlos Fuente Sr., arrived in 1980, after their factories in Nicaragua and Honduras had burned to the ground. They started modestly in Zona Franca No. 1, Santiago's first free-trade zone.</p>\n<p>By 2018 the complex had grown to four facilities in the zone, rolling about 30 million cigars a year, all by hand. About 700,000 of those, roughly 2.3 percent, were Fuente Fuente OpusX. That is the all-Dominican cigar made possible by wrapper grown at the family's farm, Chateau de la Fuente. Starting in 2015 Carlito rebuilt the main factory around a two-level courtyard known as the \"cathedral of tobacco,\" where the core <a href=\"/node/arturo/\">Arturo Fuente</a> lines are rolled. The OpusX and Don Carlos aging and rolling rooms were left untouched. Carlos Fuente Sr. died in 2016.</p>\n<p>The factory also makes contract brands, including <a href=\"/node/ashton/\">Ashton</a> and J.C. Newman's <a href=\"/node/diamondcrown/\">Diamond Crown</a>. The Don Carlos Eye of the Shark, made here, was Cigar Aficionado's 2017 Cigar of the Year. The Fuente family business is headquartered in Tampa, Florida.</p>",
  "tabolisa": "<p><strong>Tabolisa</strong> is the Estelí, Nicaragua, factory complex of <a href=\"/node/oliva/\">Oliva Cigar Co.</a> The main plant, Tabolisa I, was originally named Tabacalera Oliva de Nicaragua S.A. and has been home to Oliva since 2005.</p>\n<p>In July 2016 the Belgian company J. Cortès, owned by the Vandermarliere family, bought Oliva, which is led by <a href=\"/node/fredvandermarliere/\">Fred Vandermarliere</a>. J. Cortès then spent three years overhauling the factory. It added cigar storage and sorting space and made the packaging warehouse 17 times larger. When the remodeled Tabolisa I reopened in December 2019, capacity had risen from 55,000 to 90,000 cigars a day. The upgrade also brought a health clinic for workers and a visitors' lounge overlooking the rolling room. A second factory, Tabolisa II, opened a year earlier with capacity for about 25,000 cigars a day, and Oliva opened a new box factory in 2018. \"To let a diamond shine, you need to work it,\" Vandermarliere said.</p>\n<p>The factory rolls Oliva's flagship <a href=\"/node/seriev/\">Serie V</a> and Melanio lines along with Serie G and Connecticut Reserve, plus sub-brands <a href=\"/node/nub/\">NUB</a> and <a href=\"/node/cain/\">Cain</a>. Outside projects use the same floor: <a href=\"/node/mayflower/\">Mayflower</a> is made here, and in 2012 <a href=\"/node/padilla/\">Padilla</a> moved Padilla Reserva production to Tabolisa. Among Estelí's large family-built factories it compares in scale with <a href=\"/node/perdomofactory/\">Tabacalera Perdomo</a>.</p>",
  "perdomofactory": "<p><strong>Tabacalera Perdomo</strong> is the Estelí campus of <a href=\"/node/perdomo/\">Perdomo Cigars</a>, run by <a href=\"/node/nickperdomo/\">Nick Perdomo Jr.</a> The company is headquartered in Miami. It calls itself vertically integrated, with its own farms, greenhouses, curing barns, fermentation warehouses, sorting departments, rolling rooms, a box factory and packaging, all in Nicaragua. The main factory is nicknamed \"El Monstro.\" The complex covers about 14 acres, and Perdomo reports output of roughly 15 million cigars a year.</p>\n<p>Perdomo grows tobacco in Estelí, Condega and Jalapa. Its best-known farm, Finca Natalie in Estelí, is named for Nick and Janine Perdomo's daughter and has four curing barns that each hold 9.72 million leaves a season. Leaf is fermented in pilones of about 3,600 pounds for at least three years, under a team led by veteran tobacco man Aristides Garcia. Some wrappers are then aged in charred American white-oak bourbon barrels. Finished cigars rest six to eight months in aging rooms. The company says each cigar takes 3,054 steps and 17 inspections.</p>\n<p>Every Perdomo line is made here, from the Reserve 10th Anniversary Champagne, built on six-year-aged tobacco with bourbon-barrel-aged wrappers, to Lot 23, named for a single Estelí plot whose leaf is aged five years. Among Estelí's family-owned giants it compares with <a href=\"/node/tabolisa/\">Tabolisa</a> (Oliva) and <a href=\"/node/plasenciaesteli/\">Plasencia Estelí</a>.</p>",
  "plasenciaesteli": "<p><strong>Plasencia Estelí</strong>, known as \"the Cathedral\" for its church-like design, is the Nicaraguan factory of the <a href=\"/node/plasencia/\">Plasencia</a> family. Cigar Aficionado reports that it was built in 1996 with the German company Dannemann. <a href=\"/node/nestorandres/\">Nestor Andrés Plasencia</a> has said the family later bought out Dannemann's share and took over the factory in the early 2000s.</p>\n<p>The family's Nicaraguan story is older than the building. <a href=\"/node/nestorplasencia/\">Nestor Plasencia Sr.</a> was 14 when Cuba's land reform took his father's farm in 1963. In 1965 the family moved to Estelí, and Nestor says they were the first Cuban family to settle there. They left during the revolution in 1978, grew tobacco in Honduras, and returned to Nicaragua in 1990.</p>\n<p>The Cathedral makes the family's own Plasencia brand. Alma Fuerte, a Nicaraguan puro, debuted here in October 2016 as the first cigar of the new Plasencia push, including the hexagonally pressed Sixto, named for Nestor's father. Alma del Fuego followed. The family's Honduran factory, <a href=\"/node/plasenciahonduras/\">Plasencia Honduras</a>, is separate.</p>\n<p>The family has long made cigars for other companies as well. Cigar Nexus links some <a href=\"/node/agingroom/\">Aging Room</a> production to the Estelí factory. An older link to Rocky Patel's contract production here has not yet been confirmed by a source and is flagged for review.</p>",
  "tabacoscubanica": "<p><strong>Tabacos Cubanica</strong> is the <a href=\"/node/padron/\">Padrón</a> family's factory in Estelí, Nicaragua. <a href=\"/node/joseopadron/\">José Orlando Padrón</a> founded Padrón Cigars in Miami in September 1964 with one roller. He imported his first crop of Nicaraguan leaf in 1967 and opened a factory in Nicaragua in 1970.</p>\n<p>The Nicaraguan years were hard. In May 1978 vandals burned the Estelí factory and tobacco warehouse to the ground. Padrón rebuilt but kept an exit plan, opening a small factory in Danlí, Honduras. When the U.S. embargoed Nicaragua in 1985, the family moved its operations to Honduras. Padrón refused to use Honduran tobacco, so output fell to a third of its former level. The family returned to Estelí when the embargo was lifted in 1990, and the Honduran plant later stopped rolling cigars.</p>\n<p>Today every Padrón cigar is made by hand in Nicaragua, about 6.5 million a year as of 2014, from the core numbered line to the box-pressed <a href=\"/node/padron1964/\">1964 Anniversary Series</a> and the <a href=\"/node/padron1926/\">Serie 1926</a>, introduced on José Orlando's 75th birthday in 2001. The family grows much of its own tobacco on three owned farms and many contracted ones, and it keeps building warehouses because it treats its leaf inventory as precious. Fermentation in pilones can last a year or more. The company is now run by <a href=\"/node/jorgepadron/\">Jorge Padrón</a> with his brother <a href=\"/node/orlandopadron/\">Orlando</a>.</p>",
  "tabadom": "<p><strong>Tabadom</strong> (Tabacos Dominicanos) is where <a href=\"/node/oettinger/\">Oettinger Davidoff</a> makes its Dominican cigars, in Villa González near Santiago. Those include <a href=\"/node/davidoff/\">Davidoff</a>, <a href=\"/node/avo/\">AVO</a> and <a href=\"/node/griffins/\">The Griffin's</a>. Hendrik \"Henke\" Kelner founded it in 1984 and later sold it to Oettinger.</p>\n<p>Before Tabadom, Kelner ran cigar production at Tabacalera S.A., where with 30 to 35 rollers he made about 2 million cigars a year. They included Cacique and Vega Real for the U.S. and The Griffin's for a Geneva club. When he left to found Tabadom, he took The Griffin's with him and expanded it to six sizes. Three years later Avo Uvezian hired Tabadom to make AVO. Tabadom also made the first Ashton cigars before that brand moved to the Fuentes.</p>\n<p>Oettinger was distributing both The Griffin's and AVO when owner Ernst Schneider met Kelner in October 1988. When Davidoff left Cuba, Tabadom took over its production, starting in 1990. Kelner oversaw Davidoff blending for three decades. He handed the general manager role to Hamlet Espinal in 2015, stepped down as vice chairman in 2019 and retired in August 2020.</p>\n<p>Today Tabadom Holding runs two factories. Cigars Davidoff makes Davidoff-branded cigars, and OK Cigars makes AVO, The Griffin's, Cusano and others. An agricultural arm grows and processes tobacco. In 2025 Davidoff opened an expansion in Villa González, including a 1,700 m² blending center, that it said would double Dominican production. More than 1,200 people work at the site.</p>",
  "lagranfabrica": "<p><strong>La Gran Fábrica Drew Estate</strong> is <a href=\"/node/drewestate/\">Drew Estate</a>'s main factory in Estelí. Most accounts date its opening to July 2007, and Drew Estate's own founder history says it was fully built by September 2008. It came about when the company consolidated production from about nine smaller facilities into one campus. Drew Estate's Nicaraguan story started in late 1998, when <a href=\"/node/jonathandrew/\">Jonathan Drew</a> moved to Estelí and opened a first factory with six people in a small house. Before that, early Drew Estate cigars were made in part of Nick Perdomo's original factory.</p>\n<p>La Gran Fábrica made about 100,000 cigars a day by the mid-2010s, and roughly 160,000 a day by 2020. It is known for its colonial, Granada-style buildings and the murals by Drew Estate's in-house art studio. Brands rolled here include <a href=\"/node/acid/\">Acid</a>, <a href=\"/node/ligaprivada/\">Liga Privada</a>, <a href=\"/node/undercrown/\">Undercrown</a>, Herrera Estelí (blended by Willy Herrera, master blender since 2014), and <a href=\"/node/deadwood/\">Deadwood</a>. It also rolled the <a href=\"/node/caldwell/\">Caldwell</a> collaboration All Out Kings.</p>\n<p>Since 2014 the factory has belonged to <a href=\"/node/swisher/\">Swisher International</a> through its purchase of Drew Estate. Earlier versions of this map also listed STG brands such as Macanudo and Punch here, but we found no source for that, so those links have been removed.</p>",
  "tavicusa": "<p><strong>Tabacalera Villa Cuba S.A. (TaviCusa)</strong> is the Estelí factory owned by <a href=\"/node/rockypatel/\">Rocky Patel Premium Cigars</a>. <a href=\"/node/rakeshpatel/\">Rocky Patel</a> opened the first TaviCusa in 2009 with Amilcar Perez-Castro, in a 3,000-square-foot building. Until then his brands were made mostly in Honduras. In 2010 it moved to a much larger building just off the Pan-American Highway. That factory made 800,000 cigars in its first year and just over 2 million a year by 2014.</p>\n<p>Perez-Castro is Patel's partner and operations manager. Cuban-born, he learned tobacco in Cuba before working in Miami for <a href=\"/node/ernestoperezcarrillo/\">Ernesto Perez-Carrillo</a> and for <a href=\"/node/pepin/\">Pepín García</a> at <a href=\"/node/elreyhabanos/\">El Rey de los Habanos</a>. He trains TaviCusa's rollers in a house style of double binders, entubado bunching and triple caps, with no bunching machines. In 2014 the 8,000-square-foot factory had 72 rollers making about 10,000 cigars a day.</p>\n<p>Lines made there include the Fifteenth Anniversary (its biggest brand), Royale, Bold, Platinum, Rocky Patel 50 and Zen by <a href=\"/node/nishpatel/\">Nish Patel</a>. In 2012 <a href=\"/node/espinosa/\">Espinosa</a> moved some <a href=\"/node/sixzeroone/\">601</a> and <a href=\"/node/murcielago/\">Murciélago</a> production here. Many other Rocky Patel lines, such as The Edge, are made by the Plasencias at <a href=\"/node/plasenciahonduras/\">El Paraíso</a> in Honduras.</p>",
  "eiroadanli": "<p>The <strong>Aladino factory</strong>, also called the CLE Cigar Factory, in Danlí, Honduras, stands on the site of El Cine Aladino, a movie theater that dates to the 1970s and was run by <a href=\"/node/christianeiroa/\">Christian Eiroa</a>'s grandfather. Rollers began training in January 2013, and Eiroa's company, then called Fabricas Unidas, announced the factory fully operational in June 2013. It also makes its own boxes.</p>\n<p>The start was small: about 4,000 cigars a day from 16 pairs of hand-picked rollers, with output capped at 6,000 a day for 2014. The first lines moved there were CLE Plus, Eiroa and Asylum 13 Corojo, with plans to bring over the rest of the <a href=\"/node/cle/\">CLE</a> line and Wynwood Honduras. Asylum 13 Nicaragua, Asylum Premium, EH, Pura Soul and OSOK stayed in Nicaragua. The factory also took on outside work, starting with the boutique La Barba Cigars.</p>\n<p>Aladino uses a bunching system the <a href=\"/node/eiroafamily/\">Eiroa family</a> developed for consistency, and wrappers are stripped and applied in a way the company said had not been used in Honduras before. Eiroa opened it after the family's earlier factory, Rancho Jamastran, the home of <a href=\"/node/camacho/\">Camacho</a>, passed to Davidoff. Later releases kept their Jamastran roots. Eiroa First 20 Years (2015) was a Honduran puro made from Jamastran Valley tobacco grown by Julio Eiroa. <a href=\"/node/room101/\">Room101</a> has also had Honduran production here.</p>",
  "garmendia": "<p><strong>Garmendia Cigars</strong> is a small factory in Estelí, Nicaragua. Its owner and general manager is José Arquímides Pérez Palacios, and halfwheel identifies José Palacios as the man who runs it. The factory's own posts describe him selecting leaf in the aging room himself.</p>\n<p>Garmendia is best known on Cigar Nexus as the factory behind <a href=\"/node/westtampa/\">West Tampa Tobacco Co.</a> When <a href=\"/node/rickrodriguez/\">Rick Rodriguez</a> left General Cigar and announced West Tampa in May 2022 with partner Gus Martinez, the company's first two regular-production blends, White and Black, were both made at Garmendia. White has a Habano wrapper over Nicaraguan filler. West Tampa later added Red, and some later releases, such as Boliche Blvd and Dark Time, were made at <a href=\"/node/nacsa/\">NACSA</a> instead.</p>\n<p>Garmendia also makes cigars for retailers. Jeff Mouttet of Riverside Cigar Shop in Jeffersonville, Indiana, created Fosforo there as a store blend. He said he went looking for \"new and upcoming factories in Estelí,\" and with help from Colin Ganley found Garmendia. Fosforo grew beyond the store, and in 2023 a Fosforo Connecticut followed, with an Ecuadorian Connecticut wrapper.</p>\n<p>Garmendia is one of many small contract shops in Estelí that boutique brands and retailers use instead of building their own factories. Others on Cigar Nexus include <a href=\"/node/tabacaleraaragon/\">Tabacalera Aragon</a> and <a href=\"/node/tabacalerapages/\">Tabacalera Pages</a>.</p>",
  "hvcfactory": "<p><strong>Fábrica de Tabacos HVC S.A. de Reinier Lorenzo</strong> is the Estelí, Nicaragua, factory of <a href=\"/node/hvc/\">HVC Cigars</a>, founded by <a href=\"/node/reinierlorenzo/\">Reinier Lorenzo</a>. It opened in early 2022. That June, HVC announced the factory along with its first cigar, <strong>Selección No. 1</strong>, a Mexican San Andrés wrapper over a Nicaraguan binder and fillers from Estelí and Jalapa. Lorenzo said he wanted to pay tribute to the old Cuban style. At the time it was the only line made there, by five pairs of bunchers and rollers.</p>\n<p>Before 2022 HVC owned no factory. Its cigars were made under contract by Aganorsa Leaf at <a href=\"/node/tabsa/\">TABSA</a> (Tabacos Valle de Jalapa S.A.) in Estelí, and the map also lists some HVC production at <a href=\"/node/joyafactory/\">Joya de Nicaragua</a>. The annual Black Friday release marks the switch. The 2021 edition was still rolled at TABSA. Black Friday 2022, a 5½ x 50 robusto extra with an Ecuadorian Habano 92 wrapper and no ligero in the blend, was the first to come from HVC's own factory. Like the 2021 edition, it ran to 600 boxes of 50.</p>\n<p>Production has since moved in-house more broadly. Halfwheel's recent listings for the Hot Cake line name the HVC factory, though earlier Hot Cake cigars were made at TABSA. For Lorenzo, a former medical student from Havana, owning a factory closed a loop. \"I don't want, later in life, to regret not opening my own cigar factory,\" he told halfwheel. HVC joins <a href=\"/node/karenberger/\">Karen Berger</a> and <a href=\"/node/domain/\">Domain</a> among the Estelí brands that roll their own cigars.</p>",
  "eltitandebronze": "<p><strong>El Titan de Bronze</strong> is a family-owned, women-run cigar factory on Calle Ocho in Miami's Little Havana. Sandy Cobas started it in 1995, first in Westchester, rolling cigars for her liquor store. Cigar Snob reports that after licensing problems and her 1997 divorce she moved the operation to Calle Ocho and gave it its current name. Cobas runs it with her daughter, Giselle, and the factory describes itself as family-owned and women-operated.</p>\n<p>Its best-known outside work is for <a href=\"/node/lapalina/\">La Palina</a>, the brand revived by <a href=\"/node/billpaley/\">Bill Paley</a>. La Palina's Goldie, named for Paley's great-grandmother, was rolled at El Titan by one roller, Maria Sierra, from 2012 until she retired in 2017. Her fantail cap was the cigar's trademark. After she retired, halfwheel reported, a new roller took over.</p>\n<p>Other brands have used the factory too. <a href=\"/node/padilla/\">Padilla</a> has made Miami 8&amp;11 Maduro and Invictus there since 2012, and <a href=\"/node/warped/\">Warped</a> has had cigars made there. In 2024 <a href=\"/node/southerndraw/\">Southern Draw</a> went to El Titan for its tenth-anniversary Decennium, a 6 x 46 corona extra. That made it a rare Miami-made release for a brand otherwise rolled by A.J. Fernández in Estelí.</p>\n<p>In 2025 El Titan marked 30 years. Little Havana is also where the García family's <a href=\"/node/elreyhabanos/\">El Rey de los Habanos</a> began.</p>",
  "pdr": "<p><strong>PDR Cigars</strong> is Abe Flores's factory in the Tamboril free zone near Santiago, Dominican Republic. Flores began producing cigars in 2008 and moved into his own factory in 2011. The brand was first called Pinar del Río, but Flores dropped that name after a cease-and-desist from Habanos S.A., since everyone already called it PDR. The factory rolls in the Cuban entubado method. In 2015 it joined ProCigar as the group's 12th member, alongside <a href=\"/node/lfdfactory/\">La Flor Dominicana</a> and <a href=\"/node/laaurorafactory/\">La Aurora</a>.</p>\n<p>The house portfolio includes PDR 1878, A. Flores 1975 Gran Reserva, Flores y Rodriguez, El Criollito and AFR-75. By the mid-2010s PDR made about 5 million cigars a year, split between its own brands and contract work.</p>\n<p>Flores named several contract clients. They include <a href=\"/node/lapalina/\">La Palina</a> (Black and Classic), <a href=\"/node/gurkha/\">Gurkha</a> (125th), <a href=\"/node/kristoff/\">Kristoff</a> (Gallerones, Classic Reserve) and <a href=\"/node/viaje/\">Viaje</a> (TNT, C4). <a href=\"/node/caldwell/\">Caldwell</a> also has cigars made at PDR. The Gurkha 125th and La Palina Classic were the factory's first Cigar Aficionado Top 25 picks, which helped a young factory make its name with outside brands as much as with its own. That two-sided business, house lines plus a steady roster of clients, is how many mid-sized Dominican factories in the Santiago area operate.</p>",
  "tabacaleraaragon": "<p><strong>Tabacalera Aragón S.A.</strong> is a factory in Estelí, Nicaragua, founded by the brothers Roniel and Roidel Aragón. The factory's own history traces the family to San Juan y Martínez in Pinar del Río, Cuba, where the brothers' paternal grandparents grew tobacco, and says the brothers built the factory to carry that tradition beyond the fields. A 2022 thesis from UNAN, Nicaragua's national university, describes it as a small factory with two partners, one of them the general manager.</p>\n<p>Aragon's longest-documented client was <a href=\"/node/jassumkral/\">Jas Sum Kral</a>. halfwheel reported that Riste Ristevski moved all Jas Sum Kral production there on January 1, 2018, from <a href=\"/node/noelrojas/\">Noel Rojas</a>'s NOA. The brand left on October 11, 2024. Ristevski said he was \"no longer associated with Tabacalera Aragon S.A.\"</p>\n<p>Its main client now is <a href=\"/node/belladamacigars/\">Bella Dama Cigars</a>. Founder <a href=\"/node/chantelleavitt/\">Chantel Leavitt</a> chose Aragon after sampling with several factories in 2023, and in August 2024 the brand named Aragon the sole producer of all its cigars. Since its September 2024 relaunch, every Bella Dama line, from The King and The Queen to the budget Dealer Select range, has been rolled at Aragon.</p>\n<p>The UNAN thesis dates the company's incorporation to 2019, after halfwheel's 2018 report of Jas Sum Kral moving to a factory under the same name. The two dates may reflect a change of corporate form. We report both rather than guess.</p>",
  "tabacaleraesteli": "<p><strong>Tabacalera Estelí</strong> is the factory Enrique \"Don Kiki\" Berger founded in Estelí, Nicaragua, in 1996, at the height of the cigar boom. It started beside the Pan-American Highway, just down the road from <a href=\"/node/padron/\">Padrón</a>, with a thatch-roofed curing barn and tobacco fields next door. Berger came from a tobacco family. His father, Max Berger, had fled Poland for Cuba and set up a factory in Pinar del Río. Kiki made cigars for other companies, then in 1995 launched his own Don Kiki Red, Green, White and Brown labels. He also ran the Cuban Crafters shop in Miami, and the factory made boom-era contract brands such as Cupido before turning to his own Cuban Crafters and J.L. Salazar y Hermanos Reserva Especial.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/node/karenbergerperson/\">Karen Berger</a> joined the factory at 18 as a roller, paying her way through business school. She later married Don Kiki, and after his death in September 2014 she took over. She moved the factory into a former three-story hotel in central Estelí so its 28 artisans could work closer to their homes. She also hosts visitors there during the Puro Sabor festival, where guests wake up overlooking the production floor.</p>\n<p>The operation is vertical. About 62 acres of farmland, mostly Habano Criollo, supply all the filler, with more leaf from Jalapa and Condega, and the factory has its own curing barns. It makes about half a million cigars a year. That includes every <a href=\"/node/karenberger/\">Karen Berger Cigars</a> line, such as Don Kiki and K by Karen Berger, plus private labels for the U.S. and Europe. The 2022 25th Anniversary cigar was the exception: it was made by <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernández</a>.</p>",
  "williamventura": "<p><strong>Tabacalera William Ventura</strong> is a family-run contract factory in Tamboril, Santiago, Dominican Republic. It is led by master blender William Ventura with his children Wiber and Nataly. William ran production at Davidoff for many years, including when Davidoff cigars first came to the U.S. in 1991. Wiber spent 20 years at Davidoff before joining the family business in 2019.</p>\n<p>The factory's first major client was <a href=\"/node/caldwell/\">Caldwell</a>, about 15 years ago, and the two grew together. It has made Caldwell lines such as Eastern Standard, The King Is Dead, Long Live the King, Midnight Express, and The Last Tsar. It also rolls for J. London, Matt Booth's <a href=\"/node/room101/\">Room101</a> (Doomsayer), and STG's Partagás Y Nada Más, and has made cigars for <a href=\"/node/gurkha/\">Gurkha</a>. The Venturas also make their own William Ventura and El Maestro brands.</p>\n<p>On September 26, 2022 a fire destroyed the main factory. The family kept working from its smaller El Maestro facility and reopened a rebuilt Tabacalera William Ventura in March 2024. Earlier versions of this map also tied Viaje, Kristoff, and La Palina to this factory, but we found no source for those links, so they have been removed.</p>",
  "lucianopichardo": "<p><strong>Tabacalera Pichardo</strong> was a cigar factory in Estelí, Nicaragua, co-owned by Eradio Pichardo and Luciano Meirelles. It made cigars for outside brands. Cigar Coop named it Small/Medium Factory of the Year for 2021.</p>\n<p>Its best-known clients were <a href=\"/node/crownedheads/\">Crowned Heads</a>, whose Mil Dias was made there, and <a href=\"/node/ozgener/\">Ozgener Family Cigars</a>. Tim Ozgener's Bosphorus was first rolled at Pichardo before moving to Ernesto Perez-Carrillo's La Alianza, now <a href=\"/node/lacarrillo/\">Casa Carrillo</a>, in late 2022.</p>\n<p>In 2022 Meirelles rebranded his ACE Prime company as Luciano Cigars and moved his production to Luciano Tabacos S.A., also in Estelí. <a href=\"/node/definition/\">Definition Cigars</a>' GEN413 was made with Meirelles at Luciano Tabacos in 2023. Because sources usually follow Meirelles from one company to the next, Cigar Nexus covers the Pichardo and Luciano chapters with a single node.</p>\n<p>The story shows how often Estelí's small factories change shape. Partners split, brands rename, and clients follow the blender rather than the building. A brand's page may name Pichardo, Luciano or a newer company depending on the year. When you trace a cigar to this node, check the release date. Mil Dias and the first Bosphorus belong to the Pichardo years. GEN413 belongs to Luciano Tabacos. Other small Estelí factories on Cigar Nexus include <a href=\"/node/nicasueno/\">Nica Sueño</a> and <a href=\"/node/garmendia/\">Garmendia</a>.</p>",
  "nicasueno": "<p><strong>Fábrica de Tabacos Nica Sueño S.A.</strong> is the Estelí factory behind <a href=\"/node/romacraft/\">RoMa Craft Tobac</a>. <a href=\"/node/skipmartin/\">Skip Martin</a> and blender <a href=\"/node/estebandisla/\">Esteban Disla</a> built it together. As Martin puts it, \"Michael Rosales and I built RoMa Craft Tobac. Esteban Disla and I built Nica Sueño.\" The factory started with a six-person team in the backyard of a house in Estelí. To reach the \"factory,\" halfwheel recalled, you walked through a living room and a kitchen into the yard where two rolling teams worked.</p>\n<p>Martin dates NicaSueño to 2011, as a 2,400-square-foot building. By 2024 it had 12 rolling pairs and 67 employees in its own building, plus new pre-industry operations for preparing tobacco. It makes the RoMa Craft lines, including CroMagnon, and cigars for Powstanie. This map also records <a href=\"/node/caldwell/\">Caldwell</a> as a client.</p>\n<p>halfwheel named NicaSueño its Factory of the Year in 2020 and 2022, and in 2024 it tied for first with A.J. Fernández's <a href=\"/node/sanlotano/\">San Lotano factory</a>. Of the eight NicaSueño cigars halfwheel reviewed in 2024, six scored 90 or higher. Two of the top scorers, including the CroMagnon Knuckle Dragger, cost under $10, which reflects Martin's well-known push to keep prices low.</p>",
  "familiadisla": "<p><strong>Tabacalera Familia Disla S.A.</strong> is a factory and tobacco business in Estelí, Nicaragua, formed by <a href=\"/node/estebandisla/\">Esteban Disla</a> and <a href=\"/node/daniellance/\">Daniel Lance</a>. They announced it in March 2024 together with its house brand, <a href=\"/node/domain/\">Domain Cigars</a>.</p>\n<p>Disla began working in tobacco at age 11 in the Dominican Republic. Cigar Coop says his family was known for growing fine tobacco, with Esteban and his brother Raul helping. Esteban apprenticed under Sergio Cuevas and helped build Latin Cigars S.A., the factory now called <a href=\"/node/stgesteli/\">STG Estelí</a>, where he created blends for CAO, Toraño, Alec Bradley and some 30 other companies. He then spent just over 10 years at <a href=\"/node/nicasueno/\">Nica Sueño</a>, the factory of RoMa Craft. Lance came from cigar retail and co-founded Lujo Concepts, which makes the BTX cutter.</p>\n<p>The business started with leaf. According to its 2024 announcement, Tabacalera Familia Disla went from holding no tobacco to producing 300,000 pounds a year within five years. Filler for Domain's first blend comes from Condega and Pueblo Nuevo, fermented with the factory's own \"Despanic\" process.</p>\n<p>Domain launched with Neutron and Negentropy, both made at the factory in nine sizes, shipping from April 2024. Neutron has an aged Mexican San Andrés wrapper from TANICSA's Gilberto Oliva. Negentropy uses an Ecuadorian Habano wrapper. An earlier version of this page called Disla a former General Cigar master blender. The sources say he worked at Latin Cigars, the factory that became STG Estelí, so that claim has been corrected.</p>",
  "plasenciahonduras": "<p><strong>El Paraíso</strong> is the <a href=\"/node/plasencia/\">Plasencia</a> family's Honduran operation, the counterpart to <a href=\"/node/plasenciaesteli/\">Plasencia Estelí</a> in Nicaragua. It is named for the El Paraíso department near the Jamastrán Valley, where the family grows tobacco. The family is led by <a href=\"/node/nestorplasencia/\">Nestor Plasencia Sr.</a> and his son <a href=\"/node/nestorandres/\">Nestor Andrés Plasencia</a>, and works as both a grower and a contract manufacturer.</p>\n<p>The family came to Honduras after leaving Nicaragua in 1978, during the revolution. Its first Honduran cigar factory opened in Danlí in the mid-1980s with five rollers. Later facilities included Danlí and smaller sites in El Paraíso and Morocelí, and the family expanded its Honduran factories again in 2005.</p>\n<p>Its best-documented client is <a href=\"/node/rockypatel/\">Rocky Patel Premium Cigars</a>, which lists Plasencia's Honduran factory as the home of The Edge, Decade, Sun Grown and anniversary lines. El Paraíso also makes three of <a href=\"/node/lapalina/\">La Palina</a>'s lines, Bronze Label, Blue Label and Silver, all Honduran Habano-wrapped blends. La Palina's Black Label and Red Label are made at <a href=\"/node/pdr/\">PDR</a> in the Dominican Republic.</p>\n<p>Some older maps, including an earlier version of this one, credited El Paraíso with <a href=\"/node/tatuaje/\">Tatuaje</a> and <a href=\"/node/padilla/\">Padilla</a> production. Neither holds up. The García family has rolled every Tatuaje, and Padilla's documented factories do not include Plasencia.</p>",
  "stgesteli": "<p><strong>STG Estelí</strong> is the Nicaraguan factory of <a href=\"/node/stg/\">Scandinavian Tobacco Group</a>, where <a href=\"/node/cao/\">CAO</a>'s Nicaraguan cigars are made for <a href=\"/node/generalcigar/\">General Cigar</a>. Before STG it was Latin Cigars S.A.</p>\n<p>STG bought the factory at the start of 2009, together with a Danlí factory from the Toraño family. That was a year before STG merged with Swedish Match's cigar business, which included General Cigar. STG had already bought CAO in 2007, and after the 2010 merger General Cigar took over CAO.</p>\n<p>CAO's Nicaraguan lines, including Amazon Basin, BX3, Zócalo, Bones and Estelí, are made here, and CAO production stayed in Nicaragua rather than moving to Honduras. The map also lists newer <a href=\"/node/macanudo/\">Macanudo</a> lines and Nicaraguan <a href=\"/node/cohiba_nc/\">Cohiba</a> releases here for General Cigar.</p>\n<p>Before STG, the factory was a blending home for many outside brands. <a href=\"/node/estebandisla/\">Esteban Disla</a> worked at Latin Cigars before his ten years at Nica Sueño. Domain's 2024 launch release says he created blends there for CAO, Toraño, <a href=\"/node/alecbradley/\">Alec Bradley</a> and about 30 other companies. STG Estelí is the Nicaraguan counterpart to <a href=\"/node/stgdanli/\">STG Danlí</a> in Honduras. The map also links <a href=\"/node/room101/\">Room101</a>, <a href=\"/node/punch_nc/\">Punch</a> and <a href=\"/node/lagloriacubana/\">La Gloria Cubana</a> to this factory, but we haven't confirmed those links independently. Disla himself went on to launch <a href=\"/node/domain/\">Domain</a> with Daniel Lance in March 2024, at their own Estelí factory, Tabacalera Familia Disla.</p>",
  "stgdanli": "<p><strong>STG Danlí</strong>, listed here as HATSA (Honduras American Tabaco S.A.), is the Danlí, Honduras, factory that makes <a href=\"/node/generalcigar/\">General Cigar</a>'s Honduran <a href=\"/node/hoyonc/\">Hoyo de Monterrey</a>. Tampa cigarmaker Frank Llaneza of Villazon &amp; Co. started HATSA in Danlí around 1964, after the Cuban embargo cut off Cuban leaf. He told Cigar Aficionado that an earlier Danlí factory, started by Jimmy Corral and Julio Eiroa, didn't last, and that HATSA was for a time the only factory there. Villazon began making Hoyo de Monterrey and Punch there after buying the U.S. rights from Fernando Palicio around 1965.</p>\n<p>General Cigar bought Villazon, and with it HATSA, in 1996 for $91 million. By the late 1990s Villazon was making about 125,000 handmade cigars a day in Danlí and Cofradía, mostly Punch and Hoyo, including production for Lew Rothman's JR Cigar. Recent Hoyo releases such as Epicure Selección and the 2024 Rojo and Oscuro were rolled at the Danlí factory. General Cigar is now part of <a href=\"/node/stg/\">Scandinavian Tobacco Group</a>, which merged with Swedish Match's cigar business in 2010.</p>\n<p>STG's Honduran presence goes beyond General Cigar. At the start of 2009, STG bought two Central American factories from the Toraño and Olivas families, one in Danlí and one in Estelí, which together made about 17 million cigars a year. The Estelí plant became <a href=\"/node/stgesteli/\">STG Estelí</a>. <a href=\"/node/room101/\">Room101</a> has also worked here: in 2019 Matt Booth had The Big Payback Maduro made at HATSA, his first work in Honduras since leaving Davidoff. Non-Cuban <a href=\"/node/bolivar_nc/\">Bolivar</a> Cofradia is made here too; halfwheel and Cigar Coop put the 2024 Oscuro and 2025 Gigante releases at HATSA.</p>",
  "rojasfactory": "<p>The <strong>Rojas Cigar Factory</strong> is <a href=\"/node/noelrojas/\">Noel Rojas</a>'s factory in Estelí, Nicaragua, a converted movie theater that makes his <a href=\"/node/rojas/\">Rojas Cigars</a> and <a href=\"/node/guayacan/\">Guayacan</a> lines and <a href=\"/node/stolenthrone/\">Stolen Throne</a>. It opened in 2021 and is the third factory he has run in Nicaragua.</p>\n<p>Rojas's first was Tabacalera Aromas de Jalapa, where Guayacan was born and which he left after the partners split in late 2014. The second was <a href=\"/node/neworder/\">New Order of the Ages</a>, which he opened with Brandon Hayes on January 1, 2016, and which was reported closed by mid-2018 while Rojas planned a factory in Texas. The Rojas Cigar Factory is a different building.</p>\n<p>The theater sits just off Avenida Central in southern Estelí. Rojas leased the 7,000-square-foot space in early 2021 and gave it a rustic Texan look. It has room for about 40 pairs of rollers and a capacity of about 3 million cigars a year. Rojas planned to make all of his lines there, and since 2022 new Rojas releases have come from this floor. Rojas says all the filler and binder in his cigars comes from his own farms in Condega and Jalapa.</p>\n<p>Stolen Throne is the outside brand the factory is best known for. Rojas blends and oversees it with its owners, and the two companies have shown side by side at recent PCA trade shows.</p>",
  "raicescubanas": "<p><strong>Fábrica de Tabacos Raíces Cubanas</strong> is a factory in Danlí, Honduras, founded in May 2003 by Romay Endemaño, his wife Maria and their son Hugo. The family has roots in Cuba's tobacco trade and worked in Miami before moving to Honduras. The factory grows leaf in Trojes and, since 2016, in the Jamastrán Valley. It is best known for cigars made for other brands: many <a href=\"/node/alecbradley/\">Alec Bradley</a> lines, <a href=\"/node/illusione/\">Illusione</a>, <a href=\"/node/viaje/\">Viaje</a> and private labels. Raíces Cubanas is sometimes wrongly credited to Aganorsa's Eduardo Fernández, whose leaf it has used and who was once a client.</p>\n<p>Illusione's original portfolio was rolled entirely here until 2014, when <a href=\"/node/diongiolito/\">Dion Giolito</a> moved the core line and Maduro to <a href=\"/node/tabsa/\">TABSA</a> in Nicaragua. Epernay, Cruzado and ULTRA stayed in Danlí. The Alec Bradley tie ran deep. In 2013 Alec Bradley released a cigar called Raíces Cubanas 1941, after the year Romay was born, but it had limited success.</p>\n<p>After <a href=\"/node/stg/\">STG</a> bought Alec Bradley in 2023, former Alec Bradley partner Ralph Montero joined the Endemaños. In late 2024 they launched the factory's own brand with four lines and 18 cigars: Clásico, Liga de Reyes, C5 Black (named for the fifth priming) and the big-ring GRD3. In November 2025 they added 1941 Nuevo Comienzo, a tribute to the founders that blends Honduran and Nicaraguan tobacco.</p>",
  "laaurorafactory": "<p>The <strong>La Aurora Cigar Factory</strong> is the home of <a href=\"/node/laaurora/\">La Aurora</a>, the oldest cigar company in the Dominican Republic. Eduardo León Jimenes, then 18, opened the first factory in 1903 in the Guazumal community of Santiago province. It later moved into the city of Santiago, and in 1963 to Villa Progreso on Santiago's outskirts. In 2007 La Aurora moved all its operations back to Guazumal, where its headquarters now sit in the Tamboril free zone.</p>\n<p>The factory has grown well beyond its origins. In 2016 La Aurora said it had more than 1,100 workers and a presence in 67 markets. In 2011, the year Guillermo León bought La Aurora outright from the León Jimenes group, it also signed on to make Black &amp; Mild for John Middleton, an Altria company. The small, tourist-friendly building in Santiago where Preferidos are rolled, next to the city museum, stayed with E. León Jimenes after the sale, and it continues to make that line. Visitors to the main factory can take a tour of the tobacco process.</p>\n<p>Besides the house portfolio, the factory rolls for outside brands. <a href=\"/node/fratello/\">Fratello</a> founder Omar de Frias, who grew up in the Dominican Republic, took Fratello Oro there in 2016, and the 2026 Fratello 1821 Dominican Republic puro is made there too. La Aurora is a member of ProCigar, alongside <a href=\"/node/lfdfactory/\">La Flor Dominicana</a> and <a href=\"/node/pdr/\">PDR</a>.</p>",
  "elreyhabanos": "<p><strong>El Rey de los Habanos</strong> (\"King of the Habanos\") is the small factory <a href=\"/node/pepin/\">José \"Pepín\" García</a> founded in Miami's Little Havana after leaving Cuba in 2001. García began rolling at 11 in his uncles' factories in Báez, Villa Clara, and became a celebrated master roller. The company was founded in June 2002 and incorporated that December, and contemporary reports date the factory's opening to 2003. It sits on Calle Ocho (SW 8th Street), next door to El Credito, the birthplace of the non-Cuban La Gloria Cubana.</p>\n<p>It is one of the smallest factories in the industry, with about a dozen master rollers making roughly 800,000 cigars a year. They roll the Cuban way: no bunching machines, two binder leaves per cigar, one worker bunching and rolling each cigar, and a triple-seam cap. Its first client brand was <a href=\"/node/tatuaje/\">Tatuaje</a>. Pete Johnson's first Tatuajes were rolled here, and their high ratings put García in demand. The factory also made <a href=\"/node/padilla/\">Padilla</a> under contract, along with García's own Don Pepín García lines, starting with the Blue Label.</p>\n<p>Miami could not keep up, so the family opened Tabacalera Cubana in Estelí, Nicaragua. The business took the name <a href=\"/node/myfather/\">My Father Cigars</a> from a blend his son <a href=\"/node/jaimegarcia/\">Jaime García</a> made for him. The Calle Ocho building is still the company's corporate address and keeps rolling cigars for its own brands and clients.</p>",
  "lfdfactory": "<p><strong>Tabacalera La Flor S.A.</strong> in Tamboril, Dominican Republic, is the factory behind <a href=\"/node/lfd/\">La Flor Dominicana</a>. <a href=\"/node/littogomez/\">Litto Gomez</a> and Inés Lorenzo-Gomez started making cigars in 1994 under the name Los Libertadores. After taking over the operation in 1996, they renamed it La Flor Dominicana.</p>\n<p>The factory relies on the company's own tobacco. In 1997 Gomez bought the La Canela farm, where he grows strong ligero leaf, and much of LFD's wrapper, binder and filler comes from its own fields. That supply supports the house style: the Double Ligero line, and the Chisel, a wedge-shaped figurado introduced in 2003 that became LFD's trademark shape. The Andalusian Bull, another unusual shape from the same rollers, was Cigar Aficionado's Cigar of the Year for 2016. In 2024 the factory marked three decades with LFD 30 Years.</p>\n<p>La Flor Dominicana is a member of ProCigar, the Dominican manufacturers' association, alongside factories such as <a href=\"/node/pdr/\">PDR</a> and <a href=\"/node/laaurora/\">La Aurora</a>. Unlike contract factories such as <a href=\"/node/laisla/\">La iSLA</a>, Tabacalera La Flor exists mainly to make LFD's own cigars.</p>\n<p>The factory's output reflects the owners' choices: ligero-heavy blends from their own farm, and shapes such as the Chisel that LFD introduced. Those are why LFD is known for strength, which is not what most smokers expect of Dominican cigars.</p>",
  "lacarrillo": "<p><strong>Casa Carrillo</strong>, formerly Tabacalera La Alianza, is the factory of <a href=\"/node/ernestoperezcarrillo/\">Ernesto Perez-Carrillo</a> in a free trade zone in Santiago, Dominican Republic. It makes his house Perez-Carrillo cigars and contract cigars for other brands. He opened it in April 2009, a month after leaving General Cigar. In February 2024 the factory was renamed Casa Carrillo, and the company, <a href=\"/node/casacarrillo/\">Casa Carrillo</a> on this map, later took the same name.</p>\n<p>Perez-Carrillo's roots are on Miami's Calle Ocho, where he ran El Credito and built <a href=\"/node/lagloriacubana/\">La Gloria Cubana</a> into a major brand after strong Cigar Aficionado ratings in 1992. He expanded production to the Dominican Republic and sold El Credito to Swedish Match in 1999; its cigar business later became part of General Cigar. He stayed until March 2009, then left to make cigars on his own in Santiago.</p>\n<p>The factory makes the house Perez-Carrillo lines, including Encore, Essence and INCH, and has a long contract record. <a href=\"/node/crownedheads/\">Crowned Heads</a> has worked there for years. <a href=\"/node/caldwell/\">Caldwell</a>'s Anastasia was a collaboration with Perez-Carrillo made at La Alianza. <a href=\"/node/gurkha/\">Gurkha</a> has also used the factory. Cigar Coop named La Alianza its Small Factory of the Year for 2018.</p>\n<p>Since late 2022 the factory has also rolled <a href=\"/node/ozgener/\">Ozgener Family Cigars</a>: Bosphorus moved here from Tabacalera Pichardo, and Aramas (2023) was made here from the start. An earlier version of this map also credited the factory with Casdagli. We found no source for that, so the link has been removed.</p>",
  "laisla": "<p><strong>TABACALERA LA iSLA</strong> is a contract factory in the Gurabo free zone of Santiago, Dominican Republic. Hostos Fernández Quesada opened it in December 2020. halfwheel called that a soft opening, with 10 rolling pairs planned for January 2021. Fernández spent years in factory operations at Quesada Cigars, which his relatives own, until 2018. He then ran Cigar Rings, a company that makes cigar bands, before opening his own factory.</p>\n<p>The factory makes cigars for other companies. halfwheel lists clients including <a href=\"/node/warped/\">Warped</a>, La Sirena, Matilde, Cabal and Casa 1910. In 2023 <a href=\"/node/viaje/\">Viaje</a> began shipping New Beginnings and Mofongo, both made at La iSLA. <a href=\"/node/fratello/\">Fratello</a> also works there: its After BurnR, made with Cigar Dojo in 2023, came from La iSLA.</p>\n<p>The factory grew fast. In 2024 Fernández told L'Amateur de Cigare that La iSLA had started with a single employee and had 60, making cigars for about 40 private clients. About 90 percent of the cigars go to the United States, and the rest to Spain, Dubai and Lebanon. The same year it joined ProCigar, the Dominican manufacturers' association, an honor Fernández said showed his peers' recognition.</p>\n<p>The name was chosen to represent the country, unlike Dominican factories named after families. La iSLA belongs with a newer generation of Santiago factories, alongside <a href=\"/node/pdr/\">PDR</a>, that grew from contract work rather than a single house brand.</p>",
  "caoamerican": "<p><strong>American Caribbean Cigars</strong> is a factory in Estelí, Nicaragua, owned by Damian Tapanes and Alex Mendez. It rolls cigars for <a href=\"/node/cao/\">CAO</a>, including CAO Arcana Firewalker, and for outside brands. Earlier clients include Toraño, Leccia, and Vintage Rockefeller Cigar Group, and in 2015 it launched its own La Rosa de Sandiego line.</p>\n<p>Its biggest client has been <a href=\"/node/gurkha/\">Gurkha</a>. The partnership started around 2012 with about 150,000 cigars in the first year, and by 2016 the factory was making about 5 million Gurkhas a year. Lines include Cellar Reserve Platinum (Nicaraguan filler aged up to 12 years), Heritage Collection, Royal Challenge, Trinity, Founder's Select, and Jubilee. In May 2017 Gurkha announced it would buy the factory, but in January 2018 it cancelled the deal and said it would build its own instead.</p>\n<p>Despite the CAO work, the factory is independently owned and not part of <a href=\"/node/stg/\">Scandinavian Tobacco Group</a>. STG's own Nicaraguan plant is <a href=\"/node/stgesteli/\">STG Estelí</a>. An earlier version of this map also listed <a href=\"/node/lapalina/\">La Palina</a> production here, but we found no source for it, so that link has been removed.</p>",
  "costaricatabacos": "<p><strong>Tabacos de Costa Rica</strong> is a family-owned cigar factory in Puriscal, in the mountains west of San José. It was founded in 1997 by the elder Señor Olman and his family, and his sons Felipe and Andrés Olman now run it. German trade reports describe it as Costa Rica's largest cigar manufacturer, with a workforce that roughly tripled over 20 years. The company says it has its own tobacco plantation in Ecuador and a box factory on site, and that it makes cigars to each client's specifications.</p>\n<p>For most of its history the factory worked in the background, rolling for other brands. Its best-known client is <a href=\"/node/selectedtobacco/\">Selected Tobacco</a>, whose long-aged <a href=\"/node/atabey/\">Atabey</a> and <a href=\"/node/byron/\">Byron</a> are made here under blender <a href=\"/node/nelsonalfonso/\">Nelson Alfonso</a>. European and American brands use it too, including <a href=\"/node/lampert/\">Lampert</a> (Oro and Ocean Breeze), <a href=\"/node/casdagli/\">Villa Casdagli</a>, <a href=\"/node/lhpremium/\">LH Premium</a> and <a href=\"/node/nickandjim/\">Nick &amp; Jim</a>. So does <a href=\"/node/vegassantiago/\">Vegas de Santiago</a>, the Puriscal brand the founder helped start.</p>\n<p>Like many contract makers since the 2010s, the Olmans have also launched cigars under their own name so they don't depend only on clients. Those include the Olman Founders Edition 1997, named for the founding year, Olman Family Prestige and Malekö, shown at InterTabac in Dortmund in 2023. Visitors who tour the factory meet the family directly. A French travel guide describes Andrés as the heir and director of a factory his grandfather and father created.</p>",
  "ovejanegra": "<p><strong>Fábrica Oveja Negra</strong> is the factory that Black Label Trading Co. (BLTC) opened in Estelí, Nicaragua. Production started on February 9, 2015, with four rolling pairs and a daily limit of 200 to 250 cigars per pair to protect quality. Until then BLTC's cigars had been made by Tabacalera Santiago de Nicaragua. When the new factory opened, all of BLTC's production moved there.</p>\n<p>The name means \"black sheep,\" and the founders meant it as a promise to do things differently. \"Starting our own factory was a natural progression of the brand,\" said James Brown, BLTC's creator. \"We needed the freedom to be as creative as possible and to have ownership of every process.\" James and Angela Brown live in Nicaragua full time and run the factory.</p>\n<p>Oveja Negra was set up as a small-batch shop that also takes outside work. Brown said the goal was to help boutique brands and retailers get private-label cigars made without huge minimum orders. In 2017 it moved to a larger freestanding building on the Pan-American Highway at the south entrance of Estelí, with a production floor, tobacco storage, an aging room, a shop and a lounge.</p>\n<p>It makes BLTC's core lines and its Black Works Studio (BLK WKS) project. Its model is close to <a href=\"/node/nicasueno/\">Nica Sueño</a>, which <a href=\"/node/romacraft/\">RoMa Craft</a> built for its own cigars and for contract clients, and to small Estelí shops such as <a href=\"/node/garmendia/\">Garmendia</a>. Earlier versions of this page linked it to the Lanuza Cigar Factory. No source supports that, so the link is gone.</p>",
  "lanuzafactory": "<p><strong>Lanuza Cigars</strong> is a small, family-run factory in Estelí, Nicaragua, owned by Raúl Lanuza. The company's own site describes it as a maker of premium cigars \"mainly for private brands.\" It creates and develops blends for each client, and says it was built from the vision of \"a young tobacco worker, who always dreamed of having a cigar factory in Nicaragua.\"</p>\n<p>Retailer profiles call Lanuza a third-generation tobacco man from Estelí. They say he learned the trade at <a href=\"/node/drewestate/\">Drew Estate</a> and refined it at <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernández</a> before setting up on his own. Visitors' reviews describe a workshop of about a dozen people, away from the big factories, where Lanuza gives tours himself.</p>\n<p>The house cigars carry his name. Lanuza Habano is a toro with an Estelí-grown Habano wrapper and Condega binder and filler. Lanuza Mechudo is a robusto with a Habano Estelí wrapper over Nicaraguan tobacco. Private-label work includes TUX Cigars, whose founder Izzy Martinez has said Lanuza makes his cigars, and other small brands.</p>\n<p>Lanuza is typical of Estelí's newest tier of factories. Like <a href=\"/node/garmendia/\">Garmendia</a> or <a href=\"/node/tabacaleraaragon/\">Tabacalera Aragon</a>, it is a small, owner-run factory that depends on contract orders. Earlier versions of this page tied Lanuza to Fábrica Oveja Negra. We found no source for that, so the link has been removed.</p>",
  "tabdegarcia": "<p><strong>Tabacalera de García</strong> in La Romana, Dominican Republic, is the world's largest premium cigar factory, according to its owner, the Tabacalera group. It opened in 1969 as a modest tobacco-sorting operation, mostly handling Connecticut shade wrapper, for Consolidated Cigar, part of Gulf+Western. Its first handmade brand came in 1972 with Primo del Rey, and H. Upmann and Por Larrañaga followed. Back then it made about 500,000 cigars a year.</p>\n<p>Through a long chain of deals the factory passed to Altadis and then to Imperial Brands, which sold its premium cigar business in 2020. A new, 15-million-euro factory, built only for handmade cigars, was completed in July 2022. That year the Tabacalera group formally took it over, and the old site became Imperial Tobacco La Romana, which makes machine-made cigars. The new plant has four rolling galleries of about 240 cigarmakers each and more than 2,000 workers, and it makes about 40 million cigars a year. They include non-Cuban <a href=\"/node/montecristonc/\">Montecristo</a>, <a href=\"/node/ryjnc/\">Romeo y Julieta</a>, <a href=\"/node/hupmannnc/\">H. Upmann</a> and VegaFina.</p>\n<p>In 2025 U.S. sanctions tied to the company's majority shareholder, Chen Zhi, stopped the factory's shipments to <a href=\"/node/altadis/\">Altadis U.S.A.</a> and JR Cigar. In May 2026 OFAC granted a temporary license through 2028, and shipments resumed. Its sister factory in Honduras is <a href=\"/node/laflordecopan/\">La Flor de Copán</a>.</p>",
  "lapalma": "<p><strong>Tabacalera Palma</strong> is a cigar factory in Tamboril, near Santiago, in the Dominican Republic. <a href=\"/node/jochyblanco/\">José Arnaldo \"Jochy\" Blanco III</a> is its CEO. His father, José Arnaldo Blanco II, founded it in 1925, making it one of the country's earliest cigar manufacturers. The family's tobacco roots go back further: Jochy's great-grandfather Manuel Blanco Lozada came to the Dominican Republic from Spain in the mid-1800s.</p>\n<p>Until 1980 Palma sold only in the Dominican market. That year Jochy began making cigars for export, and in 1995 he moved the factory to Tamboril's free-trade zone as the cigar boom took off. He grows Criollo '98 and Piloto Cubano on farms in Santiago, La Canela, Gurabo, Jacagua and Mao. The house brands are La Galera, whose Maduro El Lector made Cigar Journal's Top 25 of 2017, and La Instructora, made with Aaron Saide.</p>\n<p>Palma is best known in the U.S. for <a href=\"/node/agingroom/\">Aging Room</a>, made with <a href=\"/node/rafaelnodal/\">Rafael Nodal</a>'s Boutique Blends. Early releases such as Bin No. 1 and Quattro F55 came from Palma. <a href=\"/node/gurkha/\">Gurkha</a>'s Colección Especial is also linked here. Cigar Journal lists Aging Room, Freyja, Kristoff, La Flor Dominicana, Matilde, Señorial, Trinidad Santiago and Villiger among brands that have worked with Blanco in some capacity. <a href=\"/node/kristoff/\">Kristoff</a>'s cigars themselves are made at Tabacalera Von Eicken.</p>",
  "laflordecopan": "<p><strong>La Flor de Copán</strong> is a cigar factory in Santa Rosa de Copán, western Honduras. The town hosted the Spanish colonial Royal Tobacco Factory, and tobacco has been grown in the region for centuries. In 1966 banker Jorge Bueso Arias organized a group of growers into Tabacos Hondureños S.A. to grow, cure and process leaf for export. Ten years later, in 1976, the group founded La Flor de Copán S.A. de C.V. to put a Honduran cigar on the market. It is the only factory in the area that has operated continuously since the 1970s.</p>\n<p>The family had made cigars before. In 1930 Doña Lastenia Arias de Bueso, wife of Don Manuel \"Melo\" Bueso, opened the El Encanto factory, which ran until the early 1970s. The house brands honor that history. Don Melo is named for Manuel Bueso, Encanto carries Doña Lastenia's portrait, San Fernando bears the city's coat of arms, and Santa Rosa and Hoja de Copán refer to the region. The Flor de Copán line itself dates to 1999.</p>\n<p>The factory has also worked for international companies. In the past it made <a href=\"/node/zino/\">Zino</a> for <a href=\"/node/davidoff/\">Davidoff</a>. In 2021 it said its parent company, Grupo Tabacalera, had pushed it into the Cigar Aficionado Top 25 twice: Henry Clay War Hawk was No. 10 in 2020 and San Luis Rey Carenas No. 19 in 2021. Both are <a href=\"/node/altadis/\">Altadis U.S.A.</a> brands. Its main market is the United States.</p>",
  "tabacalerapages": "<p><strong>Tabacalera Pages de Nicaragua S.A.</strong> is a cigar factory in Estelí, Nicaragua. For years it made the full portfolio of Valacari Cigars, the brand Nimmer Ahmad founded in 2016. Cigar Coop's PCA 2025 coverage still listed Pages as Valacari's factory.</p>\n<p>Through Valacari, Pages also became the first home of <a href=\"/node/lure/\">Lure Cigars</a>. Lou Cross III of Tallahassee created Lure in honor of his father, Lou Cross Jr., a lifelong fisherman and cigar smoker who died in June 2023 shortly after turning 90. Cross met Pages through Valacari, his distributor. The first Lure blend, Corojo Oscuro, soft-launched in September 2023 in ten-count bundles sealed in wax. It is a Nicaraguan puro, with a double-fermented Corojo Oscuro wrapper, an Estelí binder and filler from Estelí, Jalapa and Ometepe.</p>\n<p>That chapter ended in fall 2025. halfwheel reported that Valacari and Lure moved production from Pages to <a href=\"/node/latabacaria/\">La Tabacaria</a>, also in Estelí, as part of Valacari's tenth-anniversary overhaul. Lure's 2026 releases come from the new factory.</p>\n<p>Pages is a good example of how boutique brands use the smaller Estelí factories. A distributor such as Valacari brings in a client, and when the distributor moves, the client often moves too. Cigar Nexus maps Pages as Lure's former factory, from 2023 to 2025. For a comparable contract shop, see <a href=\"/node/garmendia/\">Garmendia</a>, which makes West Tampa.</p>",
  "latabacaria": "<p><strong>La Tabacaria Cigars</strong> is a factory in Estelí, Nicaragua. It is best known on Cigar Nexus as the current home of Valacari Cigars, the company Nimmer Ahmad founded in 2016, and of <a href=\"/node/lure/\">Lure Cigars</a>, which Valacari distributes.</p>\n<p>halfwheel reported in April 2026 that in fall 2025 Valacari and Lure moved their production from <a href=\"/node/tabacalerapages/\">Tabacalera Pages de Nicaragua</a> to La Tabacaria. The move turned out to be part of a wider overhaul for Valacari's tenth anniversary. Every Valacari line is now made in the same two sizes, a 5 x 54 robusto and a 6 x 52 toro, and packaging moved from packs of 10 to boxes of 20. The Messenger was renamed Neoclassic Connecticut, a partner to the original Neoclassic Habano. The anniversary release, Decimus, followed in 2026.</p>\n<p>Lure followed the same path. Its 2026 Catch &amp; Release Slim Jim was rolled at La Tabacaria. Lure was created by Lou Cross III in honor of his father and launched in 2023 with Corojo Oscuro, a Nicaraguan puro first made at Pages.</p>\n<p>La Tabacaria is one of the smaller Estelí factories that make cigars for brands without factories of their own. It is a different model from big rooms such as <a href=\"/node/ajfesteli/\">Tabacalera A.J. Fernández</a> or <a href=\"/node/myfather/\">My Father</a>, which run their own large house brands alongside contract work.</p>",
  "voneicken": "<p><strong>Tabacalera Von Eicken S.R.L.</strong> is a premium handmade cigar factory in the Santiago Norte free-trade zone (Zona Franca Pisano) in Santiago de los Caballeros, Dominican Republic. It was known earlier as the Charles Fairmorn factory. It belongs to Joh. Wilh. von Eicken GmbH, a German family tobacco company founded in 1770 in Mülheim an der Ruhr and now based in Lübeck, run by the eighth generation of the family.</p>\n<p>The group founded the Dominican factory in 2001, under Rolando Villamil, a tobacco veteran who ran the operation until his death in February 2022. In July 2026 the free-trade zone recognized the factory for 25 years of operations. It works from Nave 31 of the Pisano zone with about 120 direct employees, and Renso Marte is master blender and production manager. All of its output is exported, mainly to the U.S., Europe and Asia. Its own brands, Belmore and Don Antonio, are sold mostly in Europe and the Middle East.</p>\n<p>Most of its work is private-label cigars. The flagship client is Glen Case's <a href=\"/node/kristoff/\">Kristoff</a>. It is the only factory that has made Kristoff's cigars, and Case calls Villamil his mentor. The factory also makes Epic Cigars and Bronx Tale, a brand made for actor Chazz Palminteri. Other Dominican factories on this map include <a href=\"/node/lapalma/\">Tabacalera Palma</a> in nearby Tamboril and <a href=\"/node/pdr/\">PDR Cigars</a>.</p>",
};
